{"id":"settlement-firms-recurring","title":"Firms paid from the claims account year after year","question":"Which firms are paid from the City’s judgments and claims account again and again, and on which line?","finding":"The payment file shows $290.9 million paid from the judgments and claims account from fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2027 to date. $57.9 million of it went to “single payment payees” and $11.1 million to payees that read as private persons; neither is listed here. 91 firms were paid $1 million or more, or $250,000 or more across three or more years (6 public bodies that meet the same test are listed too); 55 of the firms were paid in seven or more fiscal years. 32 have a name that reads as a law firm ($70.4 million between them). On the line for the City’s own litigation fees the largest are Nossaman LLP ($7.7 million, 9 years); Beveridge & Diamond P.C. ($7.0 million, 7 years); Morgan Lewis & Bockius LLP ($6.6 million, 10 years); Renne Public Law Group LLP ($6.3 million, 10 years). On the lines for judgments and claims paid out the largest are Walkup Melodia Kelly & Schoenberger ($21.4 million); Americania Hotel ($4.7 million); The Good Hotel ($3.9 million); Greene Radovsky Maloney Share & Hennigh ($3.6 million).","how":"Payments in the Controller’s vendor-payment file whose account is “Judgments & Claims”, added up by payee. Names the project’s owner-groups tie to one owner are added together. A firm is listed when it was paid $1 million or more in all, or $250,000 or more across three or more fiscal years. “Main line” is the sub-account that holds most of its payments, as the ledger names it. “Reads as” is taken from the words in the name only (LLP, law, attorneys, professional corporation). A payment is dated by its voucher number; one that belongs to a year before fiscal 2019 is shown as 2018. Law firms and other firms are named as the file names them; private persons are never named.","limits":"Hiring outside lawyers, expert witnesses, court reporters and restoration firms is ordinary, and a firm that recurs may simply be good at one kind of case. A payment on a judgments line to a law firm is usually a settlement cheque paid into the firm’s client account for its clients, so the sum is not the firm’s fee and says nothing about the firm. The line the ledger calls “Judgements - Legal Fees” is not only fees: the settlements paid to hotel owners in 2023 and 2024 sit on it. The file’s publisher says it removes payments for judgments and claims made to individuals, so what remains is the part paid to firms. The file gives no case number, so a payment cannot be tied to a settlement the Board voted on. What would settle it: the City Attorney’s list of outside counsel agreements with rates and totals, and the claim number behind each payment. The payment file labels each line with a fiscal year that is not always the year the payment was issued: some earlier payments, mostly on purchase orders in multi-year capital funds, are filed under a later year, and the Controller's own published figures follow that label. Payments are dated here by voucher number, a single running sequence, which moves $5.52 billion of $119.3 billion (4.6%) to an earlier year than its label; $1.08 billion of that belongs to fiscal 2018 or earlier, before the file starts. Near the turn of a year the number dates a payment to within a few days.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"firm","t":"Payee","type":"org"},{"k":"reads","t":"Name reads as","type":"text"},{"k":"paid","t":"Paid, fiscal 2019–2027","type":"usd"},{"k":"years","t":"Years paid","type":"int"},{"k":"first","t":"First","type":"year"},{"k":"last","t":"Latest","type":"year"},{"k":"mainLine","t":"Main line, as the ledger names it","type":"text"},{"k":"mainShare","t":"Its share","type":"pct"},{"k":"department","t":"Mostly charged to","type":"text"},{"k":"alsoAs","t":"Also paid as","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"reads":"other firm","paid":21428418,"years":6,"first":"2019","last":"2026","mainLine":"J